# {{project.title}}

{{project.description}}.

A new axiz capability, built from a core library and Spring Boot wiring:

| Module | Layer | Contents |
|---|---|---|
| `{{platform.coreArtifact}}` | 1 | Plain Java API and implementation (no Spring) |
| `{{platform.capabilityArtifact}}-spring-boot-autoconfigure` | 3 | `{{platform.autoConfigurationClass}}` and `{{platform.propertiesClass}}` (`{{platform.prefix}}.*`) |
| `{{platform.capabilityArtifact}}-spring-boot-starter` | 3 | The dependency services add |

The core types remain under `{{project.basePackage}}`; Spring wiring lives separately under
`{{platform.springPackage}}`.

Rules: layer 1 never depends on Spring; the auto-configuration depends only on its own library
(and the foundation starter if needed); every bean is `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`; properties are an
immutable record with defaults; tests use `ApplicationContextRunner`. A capability that enters the
structure arrives through an RFC and with its use case in the demo.

The aggregator inherits `axiz-parent` to share the tool versions; each child keeps its own
layer parent. `./mvnw verify` builds the three modules. `ci.yaml` uses the Axiz 2.0.0 `lib-build` contract to
retain tested Maven bytes and a signed manifest. `deploy.yaml` independently promotes a completed
successful run with its attempt, external manifest SHA-256, environment and predecessor hash.
Builds use `GITHUB_TOKEN` with `packages: read`; isolated promotion uses its own job token
with `packages: write`. Optional `PACKAGES_READ_USERNAME/TOKEN` enable cross-repository reads. Release-please
prepares a draft which promotion publishes after retaining the verified signed manifest. `version.txt` and `.release-please-manifest.json` hold the last released version (`0.0.0`:
nothing released yet); the pom holds the next one (`0.1.0-SNAPSHOT`). The first release is `0.1.0` (`initial-version` in
`release-please-config.json`); later ones follow Conventional Commits.
